(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-oral-cavity-salivary-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Oral Cavity and Salivary Glands Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 27-year-old man has had three peritonsillar abscesses and repeated tonsillitis over two years. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Long-term prophylactic antibiotics","Repeated aspiration each episode","Quinsy tonsillectomy in all cases","Tonsillectomy once the infection settles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent tonsillitis with recurrent abscesses is the indication for tonsillectomy, and most surgeons prefer to operate after the acute inflammation has resolved, because the planes are cleaner and bleeding is less. Quinsy tonsillectomy during infection is reserved for particular circumstances. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has painless white lesions on the lateral border of the tongue that wax and wane. He has no known medical conditions. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Prescribe a topical antifungal","Reassure and discharge","Test for HIV","Excisional biopsy to exclude carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hairy leukoplakia is a benign mucosal hyperplasia driven by Epstein-Barr virus and is strongly associated with HIV, where it is a common early finding. The lesion itself needs only observation, so its clinical value lies almost entirely in what it points to. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 136"},{"q":"An 11-year-old has two days of sore throat with a temperature of 101 degrees, tonsillar exudate and tender anterior cervical nodes. She has no cough. The rapid antigen test is negative. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Send a throat culture before deciding","Accept the negative test and treat symptomatically","Start penicillin without further testing","Order antistreptolysin O titres today"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her Centor score is high, with a point each for absent cough, exudate, fever and tender nodes, plus one for her age. Rapid antigen tests are specific but insufficiently sensitive, so a negative result in a child with high clinical probability is always confirmed by culture. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has painless oral erosions with surrounding erythema and honeycomb patches on the buccal mucosa. She has photosensitivity and joint pains. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Aphthous stomatitis","Behcet syndrome","Oral lichen planus","Oral involvement of systemic lupus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 40 per cent of lupus patients have mucous membrane involvement, and oral lesions may be the FIRST sign of the disease. Lesions include cheilitis, erythematous patches, honeycomb patches and discoid ulcers, and there is no correlation with systemic activity. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 19-year-old student treated with amoxicillin for a presumed streptococcal sore throat develops a widespread maculopapular rash three days later. Her tonsils are enlarged with exudate and she has bilateral cervical adenopathy and marked fatigue. What is the correct explanation?","choices":["An unrelated viral exanthem","Mononucleosis, with the rash triggered by the aminopenicillin","A true penicillin allergy requiring lifelong avoidance","Scarlet fever from the streptococcal infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Giving an aminopenicillin in Epstein-Barr infection reliably provokes a widespread exanthem, and this sequence is the classic route to the diagnosis. The triad of fever, tonsillar pharyngitis and cervical lymphadenopathy fits mononucleosis rather than streptococcus. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has severe sore throat, odynophagia and a muffled voice for a day. He is febrile but not drooling and has no stridor. Laryngoscopy shows a swollen epiglottis. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Admit for observation and intravenous antibiotics","Immediate tracheostomy","Discharge on oral antibiotics","Lateral neck X-ray before treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adult supraglottitis progresses more slowly than the paediatric form and often does not need immediate intubation, so observation with humidification, glucocorticoids, intravenous antibiotics and nebulised adrenaline is appropriate, with the airway monitored in case it obstructs. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man who smokes and drinks has an ulcer on the floor of the mouth that has not healed in six weeks. He reports right ear pain, and his ear examination is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Oral candidiasis","Traumatic ulcer from a denture","Oral cavity squamous cell carcinoma","Aphthous ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A non-healing ulcer in a patient with tobacco and alcohol exposure is the classic presentation, and squamous cell carcinoma is the commonest oral malignancy. The referred otalgia with a normal ear reflects shared innervation between the oral cavity and the ear, and indicates more advanced disease. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 146"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with dentures complains they no longer fit, and she has vague discomfort in the mouth and a foreign body sensation in the throat. What should be suspected?","choices":["Possible oral or oropharyngeal malignancy","Normal denture wear requiring adjustment","Temporomandibular joint disorder","Oral candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ill-fitting dentures are on the list of presenting features alongside non-healing ulcers, bleeding, pain, vague discomfort and a foreign body sensation in the throat. Advanced disease adds dysarthria, dysphagia, a neck mass and referred otalgia, so an apparently minor complaint deserves a proper look. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 146"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has two days of fever, sore throat, neck stiffness and refusal to eat, and is now drooling and leaning forward with the neck extended. Lateral neck X-ray shows widened prevertebral soft tissue. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Epiglottitis","Peritonsillar abscess","Bacterial pharyngitis","Retropharyngeal abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Suppuration of retropharyngeal nodes after an upper respiratory infection is a disease of children under five, and widening of the prevertebral soft tissue is the classic radiographic sign. It is a surgical emergency because the space runs to the posterior mediastinum, where mediastinitis carries 50 per cent mortality. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has a tonsillar squamous cell carcinoma that is human papillomavirus positive. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Surgical resection with radiotherapy","Surgical resection alone","Chemotherapy alone","Observation, since virus-related tumours regress"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oropharyngeal tumours are treated with resection plus radiotherapy, which gives better functional outcomes in a site where speech and swallowing are at stake. Between 60 and 80 per cent of oropharyngeal cancers are human papillomavirus related, which also affects prognosis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has fever and sore throat with small ulcerative vesicles over the tonsils and soft palate. He also has a cough and coryza. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Viral pharyngitis with herpangina","Streptococcal pharyngitis","Diphtheria","Oral candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Herpangina is ulcerative vesicles over the tonsils and is a feature of viral pharyngitis, usually coxsackievirus. The accompanying cough and coryza reinforce a viral cause, and absence of cough is a Centor point precisely because its presence argues against streptococcus. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 15-year-old is referred with an abnormal bite. The clinician classifies the occlusion. What is the correct classification?","choices":["Class I mesial, Class II normal, Class III distal","The classes describe crowding","The classes describe missing teeth","Class I normal, Class II distal, Class III mesial"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The classes describe how the lower dental arch sits relative to the upper: Class I is normal alignment, Class II is distal occlusion with the lower arch set back, and Class III is mesial occlusion with it forward. Consistent naming is what allows the problem to be communicated to an orthodontist. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 126"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy who shouts constantly during sport has been hoarse for four months. Laryngoscopy shows small, whitish, bilateral symmetric lesions at the junction of the anterior third and posterior two thirds. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Speech therapy","Surgical excision of both nodules","Inhaled corticosteroid","Antireflux therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vocal cord nodules are the commonest cause of persistent dysphonia in children, sometimes called screamers' nodules, forming where the folds strike hardest during phonation. Because the cause is vocal behaviour, speech therapy that changes it is first line in both children and adults. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman notices small yellow-white papules on her upper lip vermilion and inside her cheek. They are painless and she is otherwise well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Oral candidiasis","Leukoplakia","Herpes labialis","Fordyce granules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are ectopic sebaceous glands appearing where sebaceous glands are not usually expected, and they are a normal variant. Patients often find them alarming because they notice them suddenly, but they have always been there and need only recognition and reassurance. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man using an inhaled corticosteroid has white patches on the buccal mucosa and tongue. Wiping one with a tongue depressor removes it, leaving an erythematous base. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Leukoedema","Oral candidiasis","Oral leukoplakia","Oral lichen planus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Candida forms a removable pseudomembrane, so wiping it reveals inflamed mucosa beneath. Inhaled corticosteroid deposited in the mouth suppresses local immunity, which is why patients are told to rinse afterwards, and why this is a common cause in someone otherwise well. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 7-year-old unimmunised boy has bilateral painful parotid swelling with fever and malaise. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute suppurative sialadenitis","Sialolithiasis","Salivary gland neoplasm","Mumps"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mumps is caused by a paramyxovirus and is the classic viral cause of parotitis, typically bilateral with systemic symptoms in an unimmunised child. Other causes of parotitis include herpes and Epstein-Barr virus, bacterial infection, diabetes, tumours, stones and dental problems. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 24-year-old with a peritonsillar abscess can barely open his mouth two centimetres. What is this called?","choices":["Tetanus","Trismus","Mediastinal spread","Vagal involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pus tracking beside the medial pterygoid irritates it into spasm, limiting mouth opening. Of the classic triad of trismus, uvular deviation and dysphonia, trismus is singled out as the most reliable, and it is also what makes examination and drainage difficult. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man who has chewed tobacco for thirty years has a white plaque on the lateral tongue that will not scrape off and has been present four months. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid for presumed lichen planus","Excisional biopsy","Topical antifungal and review in two weeks","Reassurance, as white oral lesions are usually benign"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leukoplakia is premalignant by definition, with 5 to 20 per cent progressing to squamous cell carcinoma, and appearance alone cannot identify which lesions harbour dysplasia. Biopsy is the diagnostic step, alongside eliminating the carcinogenic irritant and examining for lymphadenopathy. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 134"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has a dental abscess. The tooth is badly broken down and cannot be restored. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Extraction with curettage of the apical tissue","Root canal treatment","Antibiotics alone until it settles","Incision and drainage alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management depends on whether the tooth can be saved: a restorable tooth gets a root canal, and one that cannot be restored is extracted, with the apical tissue curetted because leaving infected granulation tissue allows the infection to persist in the socket. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a parotid mass with pain and a partial facial nerve palsy on the same side. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A benign pleomorphic adenoma","Acute suppurative sialadenitis","Sialolithiasis","A malignant salivary tumour, with a poor prognosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prognosis is poor when there is pain, facial or other nerve involvement, high-grade histology, skin or tissue invasion, or recurrent disease. Facial nerve palsy in particular indicates invasion rather than compression, and management is surgical removal with radiotherapy for certain stages. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 143"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a breathy hoarse voice and coughs when drinking, six weeks after thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy shows one immobile cord. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vocal cord nodules from postoperative strain","Post-intubation laryngeal oedema","Unilateral paralysis from recurrent laryngeal nerve injury","Bilateral vocal cord paralysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs immediately behind the thyroid and is at risk during thyroidectomy. A unilateral paralysis leaves one cord unable to meet the other, giving a breathy voice from air escape and allowing aspiration during swallowing. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has gums that are red, swollen and bleed when he brushes, with little discomfort. There is no pocketing or tooth mobility. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Gingivitis, reversible with cleaning and home care","Periodontitis with irreversible bone loss","A dental abscess","Oral lichen planus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gingivitis is the mildest form of periodontal disease: inflammation confined to the gingiva from plaque at the gum line, giving erythema, oedema and bleeding with little discomfort. The critical point is that it is fully reversible at this stage, before the periodontal ligament and bone are destroyed. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 121"},{"q":"A 20-year-old student has recurrent painful oral ulcers with yellow-grey centres and red halos, each under a centimetre, on the inner cheek and lip. They heal in about a week and recur during examinations. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpetic gingivostomatitis","Herpetiform aphthous ulceration","Minor aphthous ulceration","Major aphthous ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Minor aphthae are under a centimetre, are the commonest form, burn and tingle before appearing, and heal in 7 to 10 days without scarring. Their location on freely moving non-keratinised mucosa is diagnostic, and stress is a recognised precipitant. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man who smokes heavily and shouts at work has six months of hoarseness. Laryngoscopy shows a single pedunculated lesion on the left cord with visible vascular markings. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["A course of oral corticosteroids","Microlaryngoscopic excision","Speech therapy alone","Observation with repeat laryngoscopy in a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A polyp is unilateral and pedunculated, which distinguishes it from bilateral symmetric nodules, and is commoner in men who smoke and abuse the voice. Excision both treats it and provides tissue, which matters because a large polyp may conceal an occult early laryngeal squamous carcinoma in exactly this risk group. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 15-year-old has inflammation of both the tonsils and the throat. What is this called?","choices":["Laryngitis","Pharyngotonsillitis","Tonsillitis only","Pharyngitis only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The three terms describe which structure is inflamed: tonsillitis if the tonsils, pharyngitis if the throat, and pharyngotonsillitis if both. They are not three different diseases, and the same organisms cause all of them, but tonsillar involvement raises the possibility of complications such as peritonsillar abscess. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 69"},{"q":"A pregnant woman at 30 weeks asks about vaccination. What is the correct advice?","choices":["In the first trimester","Immediately after delivery","Tdap now, between 27 and 36 weeks","Only before the first pregnancy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tdap is recommended in each pregnancy between 27 and 36 weeks. Vaccinating in that window maximises transplacental antibody transfer, protecting the newborn during the months before the infant's own schedule takes effect. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman who smokes and drinks has a well-demarcated red velvety patch on the floor of the mouth that does not scrape off. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Geographic tongue","Erythroplakia","Leukoplakia","Erythematous candidiasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Erythroplakia is the red counterpart of leukoplakia and is far more dangerous: about 90 per cent are already dysplastic or frankly malignant, against 5 to 20 per cent for leukoplakia. Alcohol and tobacco are the major risk factors and it is biopsied urgently. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 135"},{"q":"A 22-year-old has a sore throat with cough, hoarseness, rhinitis and conjunctivitis for three days. She is afebrile with no tonsillar exudate. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Empiric penicillin for 10 days","Throat culture and admit for observation","Supportive care with no further testing","Rapid antigen test and treat if positive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cough, rhinitis, hoarseness and conjunctivitis all point to viral pharyngitis, which accounts for about 70 per cent of cases. Her Centor score is minimal, so testing and antibiotics are not indicated, and management is hydration, antipyretics and analgesia. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 9-year-old recently arrived from a region with low immunisation coverage has a mild sore throat, low fever and marked malaise. A tenacious grey membrane covers the tonsils and pharynx and bleeds when disturbed. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Supportive care for presumed viral pharyngitis","Corticosteroids to reduce swelling","Antitoxin from the Centers for Disease Control plus antibiotics","Penicillin alone for 10 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An adherent grey membrane in an unimmunised child is diphtheria. The damage is caused by an exotoxin producing myocarditis and cranial neuropathy, so antitoxin is required to neutralise circulating toxin, alongside penicillin or erythromycin for 14 days and isolation until three consecutive cultures are negative. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has severe toothache. The clinician explains what caused the pulpitis. What is the commonest cause?","choices":["Malocclusion","Tooth decay, with injury second","Injury, with decay second","Impaction of the tooth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The commonest cause of pulpitis and periapical abscess is tooth decay, and the second commonest is injury. Mild inflammation may resolve without permanent damage, but severe inflammation kills the pulp, and a pocket of pus may then form at the root. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 124"}]};
